130	## Performance scenarios and report
131	
132	`npm run test:performance` builds first, then uses the MCP SDK
133	`StdioClientTransport` to spawn the real `dist/cli.mjs` with `process.execPath`.
134	Build time is therefore outside every latency sample. A child-only Node
135	`--import` preload installs deterministic Nock fixtures before the CLI loads,
136	requires the dedicated fake API key, disables Node HTTP(S) connections, and
137	rejects `globalThis.fetch` so the background update check cannot contact npm.
138	The expected blocked npm-registry URL is recorded; any other fetch target is an
139	unexpected request and fails fixture verification. It never contacts live Hevy.
140	Issue #609 remains responsible for the broader installed-tarball expansion.
141	
142	The lane records exactly five stable scenarios:
143	
144	1. `startup-initialization` — 10 process launches through MCP initialize.
145	2. `mcp-tools-list` — 20 MCP `tools/list` calls on one initialized process.
146	3. `representative-mocked-read` — 20 child-mocked `get-workout` calls.
147	4. `concurrent-20-call-burst` — one burst of 20 correlated mocked workout reads.
148	5. `sequential-100-mocked-reads` — 100 ordered mocked reads.
149	
150	The versioned JSON report is written to the ignored stable path
151	`test-results/performance/summary.json`. It includes the commit, Node/runtime
152	environment, platform, architecture, CPU/runner metadata, fixture/network mode,
153	configured/completed iteration counts, p50/p95/max durations, correctness
154	failures, exact child fixture verification, and server-process RSS observations
155	from `/proc/<pid>/status` on Linux (nullable with an explicit reason elsewhere).
156	Parent/runner memory is labeled separately.
